WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.240
Right now, at this exact moment, your brain is actively destroying information.

00:06.580 --> 00:08.040
Just massive, massive amounts of it.

00:08.080 --> 00:09.280
Just obliterating it.

00:09.600 --> 00:13.600
And it is doing this, just so you can understand the sentence I'm speaking right now,

00:13.800 --> 00:16.500
I mean, it's deleting the hum of the refrigerator in the background,

00:17.020 --> 00:19.140
the feeling of your shoes on your feet.

00:19.240 --> 00:22.000
The memory of whatever you had for breakfast, you know, three weeks ago.

00:22.040 --> 00:22.980
Exactly. It's all gone.

00:23.200 --> 00:24.760
And it actually has to be that way.

00:24.760 --> 00:29.580
Because if your mind, or really any system for that matter, kept absolutely everything,

00:30.000 --> 00:33.520
it would instantly collapse under the crushing weight of its own history.

00:33.900 --> 00:33.980
Right.

00:34.200 --> 00:40.080
Every single act of remembering actually requires this massive, continuous act of forgetting.

00:40.540 --> 00:43.400
It is a terrifying thought when you really sit with it for a minute.

00:43.400 --> 00:43.880
Oh, totally.

00:44.100 --> 00:47.480
Because, I mean, we rely on our memories to tell us who we are, right?

00:47.700 --> 00:51.400
And we rely on historical records to tell us what happened in the world.

00:51.400 --> 00:57.520
But the survival of literally any system depends on throwing almost all of that information away.

00:57.980 --> 00:59.960
Which introduces a pretty massive paradox.

01:00.000 --> 01:04.680
Right, because if the past is constantly being compressed and just discarded,

01:04.980 --> 01:08.460
how can we ever confidently know what actually happened?

01:08.660 --> 01:10.660
And that paradox is our mission today.

01:10.900 --> 01:16.940
We are diving into a really dense, completely paradigm-shifting 2026 paper

01:16.940 --> 01:18.740
by a researcher known as Fliction.

01:19.120 --> 01:25.500
It's titled Compressed Causality, Stability, Projection, and the Geometry of Alternative Histories.

01:25.700 --> 01:26.480
It's quite a title.

01:26.480 --> 01:27.360
It is, yeah.

01:27.760 --> 01:33.540
But the goal of this deep dive today is to extract this incredible unified framework from it.

01:33.900 --> 01:39.320
We want to understand how systems reconstruct lost histories from these heavily compressed,

01:39.660 --> 01:40.920
zipped-up present states.

01:41.180 --> 01:43.220
And we are talking about a huge range of things here.

01:43.320 --> 01:48.000
Everything from how an artificial intelligence answers questions to human memory and trauma.

01:48.460 --> 01:50.020
Legal systems building on precedent.

01:50.140 --> 01:53.180
Yeah, all the way out to the cosmological limits of the universe itself.

01:53.180 --> 01:57.540
So I want to start by tearing down an assumption that I definitely had before I started reading this text.

01:57.640 --> 01:58.360
Okay, what was it?

01:58.640 --> 02:04.380
Well, I always thought that an incomplete record of the past was just, you know, a failure of technology.

02:04.720 --> 02:05.960
Or a failure of biology.

02:05.980 --> 02:07.080
Like a bug in the system.

02:07.520 --> 02:07.920
Exactly.

02:08.540 --> 02:12.280
Like if we just had a big enough hard drive, or if humans had photographic memories,

02:12.480 --> 02:13.720
we could just keep the whole truth.

02:14.100 --> 02:16.280
But Fliction comes right out of the gate and says no.

02:16.500 --> 02:17.340
No, not at all.

02:17.340 --> 02:23.060
An incomplete record is the required normal condition for any system existing in the present.

02:23.460 --> 02:23.780
Right.

02:23.900 --> 02:26.780
The central realization here is that this isn't just a metaphor.

02:27.080 --> 02:31.560
We aren't just comparing human memory to, like, a computer hard drive.

02:32.220 --> 02:35.360
Fliction is presenting a universal mathematical law here.

02:35.520 --> 02:36.520
Which is wild.

02:36.600 --> 02:37.120
It is.

02:37.120 --> 02:43.560
The underlying geometry of how a present state holds on to its past is identical across all these domains.

02:43.560 --> 02:48.920
So whether you are looking at the parameters of a machine learning model, the legal code of a nation.

02:49.180 --> 02:52.100
Or the cosmic microwave background radiation hitting a satellite.

02:52.240 --> 02:52.720
Exactly.

02:52.880 --> 02:54.780
Or the neural pathways firing in your head.

02:55.160 --> 02:58.880
They are all bound by the exact same laws of compressed causality.

02:59.320 --> 03:05.360
Because the sheer volume of a true uncompressed history is just impossible to carry forward.

03:05.580 --> 03:05.780
Right.

03:06.020 --> 03:08.500
Think about what a true history actually entails.

03:08.860 --> 03:10.300
Take a machine learning model, for instance.

03:10.300 --> 03:13.940
It might be trained on trillions of words, billions of images.

03:14.080 --> 03:15.320
Just mountains of data.

03:15.620 --> 03:15.920
Mountains.

03:16.740 --> 03:23.440
But when the training phase is over, the model doesn't actually store a giant library of all those original images and texts.

03:23.660 --> 03:25.320
It only stores its parameters.

03:25.700 --> 03:27.140
The mathematical weights and biases.

03:27.420 --> 03:27.600
Right.

03:27.720 --> 03:32.060
The raw history, the actual training data is permanently gone.

03:32.440 --> 03:33.860
And it's exactly the same with us.

03:33.860 --> 03:40.840
I mean, you listening right now, you've been accumulating sensory data every millisecond since the day you were born.

03:41.840 --> 03:48.600
But you don't possess a continuous, unedited video file of your life in your head.

03:48.760 --> 03:49.860
That would be exhausting.

03:50.180 --> 03:50.920
It would be impossible.

03:51.240 --> 03:57.440
Your brain takes this chaotic torrent of daily experience and squishes it down into these little conceptual summaries.

03:57.600 --> 04:00.600
And physical reality does this too, which is the craziest part.

04:00.840 --> 04:02.720
Yeah, explain that because that blew my mind.

04:02.720 --> 04:10.140
Well, if you have a cup of coffee sitting on your desk, the present state of that coffee, its temperature, its volume, that is a compression.

04:10.420 --> 04:11.300
A compression of what?

04:11.500 --> 04:12.360
Of its history.

04:12.540 --> 04:24.040
The universe doesn't actively encode the microscopic trajectory of every single water molecule and coffee bean particle spanning back years just to maintain the current temperature.

04:24.900 --> 04:31.880
A really complex microscopic history has been replaced by a compressed macroscopic representation.

04:31.880 --> 04:36.440
Okay, but the problem is, we don't just accept that the past is gone, right?

04:36.660 --> 04:38.620
We are deeply obsessed with it.

04:38.660 --> 04:42.020
We are constantly asking questions about the history that was lost.

04:42.280 --> 04:42.620
Always.

04:42.760 --> 04:46.400
I mean, I might ask, what would I be doing right now if I hadn't moved to this city 10 years ago?

04:46.800 --> 04:54.100
Or, you know, an engineer might ask, what would this AI output look like if we had removed one specific toxic document from its training data?

04:54.100 --> 04:59.600
Right. We are constantly querying a compressed state, demanding answers about a deleted history.

05:00.020 --> 05:07.560
So to explain how we even attempt to answer those questions, Fliction introduces a mathematical structure built on three distinct spaces.

05:07.820 --> 05:11.500
And understanding these three spaces is really the key to the entire framework.

05:11.720 --> 05:13.540
Okay, so let's break them down. Space number one.

05:13.540 --> 05:19.540
The first is what the text calls the trajectory space, or mathematically, it uses the variable X.

05:19.940 --> 05:27.680
I found this concept incredibly beautiful, honestly. The trajectory space. It's essentially the ultimate God's eye view of reality, right?

05:27.840 --> 05:38.000
It is the ontological space. So in philosophy, ontology deals with what actually exists. The trajectory space is the space of all possible histories.

05:38.360 --> 05:40.180
The raw, uncompressed truth.

05:40.180 --> 05:51.860
Exactly. A single trajectory in this space is a totally uncompressed, flawlessly exhaustive record of what actually happened. Every atom, every thought, every single variable.

05:52.040 --> 05:56.400
But this is the kicker. The trajectory space also contains every alternative history.

05:56.600 --> 06:00.040
Yes. Every possible way the system could have evolved but didn't.

06:00.200 --> 06:08.160
So it's the space of everything that ever was and everything that ever could have been. But because no system can carry that around, we have to move to the second space.

06:08.160 --> 06:10.240
Right. The representation space, or M.

06:10.320 --> 06:11.480
Which is the epistemic space.

06:11.520 --> 06:18.860
Yeah. Epistemology is the study of knowledge. So this space represents what we actually know in the present. It's the compressed state.

06:18.940 --> 06:19.520
The zip file.

06:19.780 --> 06:24.820
The zip file. Exactly. It's the neural connections in your brain or the weights in the AI model.

06:24.820 --> 06:33.720
And to get from that massive, infinite trajectory space down to this manageable representation space, the system utilizes a mechanism.

06:33.920 --> 06:34.820
The compression operator.

06:35.040 --> 06:40.760
Right. And this operator doesn't just randomly delete things. It does something very specific mathematically.

06:41.540 --> 06:45.660
The text says it partitions the trajectory space into fibers.

06:45.980 --> 06:48.860
Okay. Fibers. This is where it gets a little abstract, but it's so important.

06:48.860 --> 06:59.840
It is the engine of this entire theory. A fiber is a cluster of alternative histories that, from the perspective of the compressed present, are completely indistinguishable from one another.

06:59.960 --> 07:08.060
Okay, wait. Let me make sure I have this. If two totally different pasts end up being sorted into the exact same fiber by the compression operator...

07:08.060 --> 07:15.720
Then it means you can never look at the present state and tell which of those two pasts actually happened. They've been mathematically forced into equivalence.

07:15.720 --> 07:19.700
Okay. Let me test out an analogy here to see if I have the mechanics of this right.

07:20.360 --> 07:28.040
Let's say my compressed representation, my present knowledge, is just the memory that I ate a sandwich for lunch yesterday.

07:28.100 --> 07:28.940
Okay. A sandwich.

07:29.480 --> 07:33.040
That concept, sandwich, is my representation space.

07:33.500 --> 07:40.040
Now, if we look back at the trajectory space, there is a literal uncompressed history where I ate a turkey sandwich.

07:40.160 --> 07:40.420
Right.

07:40.420 --> 07:46.060
But there's also an alternative history where I ate a ham sandwich, and another where it was a veggie wrap.

07:46.300 --> 07:46.740
Yes.

07:46.920 --> 07:57.300
Because my brain's compression operator threw away the specific meat data and only kept the broad category sandwich, all of those specific pasts are lumped into a single fiber.

07:57.400 --> 07:59.100
That is the perfect way to visualize it.

07:59.100 --> 08:04.260
So based purely on my memory today, the turkey past and the ham past are equally valid realities.

08:04.700 --> 08:05.900
They are trapped in the same fiber.

08:06.220 --> 08:10.060
I literally cannot tell them apart using only the compressed data I saved.

08:10.260 --> 08:11.360
You've lost the resolution.

08:11.920 --> 08:16.260
And that leads us to the third space, the query space, or R.

08:16.740 --> 08:19.080
This is where we actually try to use the information we kept.

08:19.180 --> 08:20.060
The pragmatic space.

08:20.220 --> 08:20.440
Right.

08:20.640 --> 08:22.380
Where we ask counterfactual questions.

08:22.760 --> 08:25.580
A query is you trying to probe that fiber.

08:25.740 --> 08:28.080
So my query is, did I eat turkey yesterday?

08:28.080 --> 08:31.680
And here is the reconstruction problem, the central dilemma of the paper.

08:32.380 --> 08:40.540
Under what conditions can your system accurately answer that query using only a compressed representation that doesn't explicitly contain the answer?

08:40.720 --> 08:44.040
How do you pull the turkey out of a memory that only says sandwich?

08:44.560 --> 08:44.860
Right.

08:45.060 --> 08:46.820
Intuition screams that you can't.

08:47.280 --> 08:49.260
If the data's gone, it's just gone.

08:49.260 --> 08:56.060
Our intuition definitely tells us it's impossible, but intuition fails to explain the exact mechanics of the failure.

08:56.460 --> 09:01.300
And knowing why we fail is literally the only way to figure out how to succeed.

09:01.660 --> 09:03.640
So Flick can formalize this failure.

09:03.860 --> 09:04.140
Yes.

09:04.360 --> 09:07.260
Into what is called the fundamental error decomposition.

09:07.960 --> 09:12.500
This is where the text really shifts from like philosophy to hard mechanics.

09:12.500 --> 09:18.480
When we try to reconstruct the past to get it wrong, Flickson proves it is never just some generalized fuzziness.

09:18.680 --> 09:19.700
It's highly specific.

09:19.900 --> 09:24.800
The failure is always the sum of three completely independent mathematically distinct errors.

09:25.060 --> 09:29.200
So let's break those down because they are deeply revealing about how the world works.

09:29.700 --> 09:31.980
The first one is compressibility error.

09:32.520 --> 09:34.980
The text calls this a structural failure.

09:35.200 --> 09:39.020
Meaning it's not a glitch in our technology or our brains.

09:39.520 --> 09:41.300
It's a flaw in the universe itself.

09:41.300 --> 09:42.600
Essentially, yes.

09:43.000 --> 09:54.060
A compressibility failure happens when the answer to your query relies on incredibly complex, highly distant historical interactions that simply refuse to fade away over time.

09:54.180 --> 09:55.140
A butterfly effect.

09:55.300 --> 09:55.640
Basically.

09:56.160 --> 10:05.900
If the outcome in the present is highly sensitive to the microscopic chaotic details of something that happened years ago, then no finite compression can ever capture that history.

10:06.060 --> 10:07.380
Because you'd need too much data.

10:07.560 --> 10:10.280
You would have to save the whole universe just to answer the question.

10:10.280 --> 10:13.640
I was trying to map this onto a real world scenario.

10:13.940 --> 10:19.780
Imagine you're trying to reconstruct a recipe for this incredible loaf of sourdough bread you had last month.

10:19.860 --> 10:20.040
Okay.

10:20.180 --> 10:20.720
Baking bread.

10:20.880 --> 10:33.060
A compressibility error would be if the success of the dough relied on knowing the exact microscopic temperature of a specific raindrop that watered a single stalk of wheat in a field three months prior.

10:33.060 --> 10:41.340
It is structurally impossible to track, store, and reconstruct that level of distant chaotic interaction.

10:41.800 --> 10:43.720
The history simply isn't compressible.

10:43.920 --> 10:46.340
That perfectly isolates the nature of the error.

10:46.680 --> 10:49.120
It's an issue with the history itself, not your notebook.

10:49.320 --> 10:51.360
You literally couldn't record it if you tried.

10:51.500 --> 10:51.660
Yeah.

10:51.800 --> 10:52.000
Okay.

10:52.080 --> 10:52.900
So that's structural.

10:53.240 --> 10:57.180
But the second type of error is sufficiency error.

10:57.420 --> 10:58.680
And this one is our fault.

10:58.820 --> 11:00.060
It's an architectural failure.

11:00.060 --> 11:03.180
This is a design flaw in how we chose to compress the data.

11:03.420 --> 11:03.820
Exactly.

11:04.440 --> 11:08.860
A sufficiency failure occurs when the history actually could have been compressed effectively.

11:09.240 --> 11:10.860
The universe allowed for it.

11:11.180 --> 11:15.640
But your specific compression operator just threw away the wrong information.

11:15.980 --> 11:16.800
You kept the wrong stuff.

11:16.880 --> 11:21.320
You preserved a structure, sure, but not the structure your query actually needed.

11:21.520 --> 11:25.520
So back in the kitchen, a sufficiency error means you wrote down the entire recipe.

11:25.520 --> 11:31.060
You diligently recorded the weight of the flour, the hydration percentage, the mixing time.

11:31.180 --> 11:32.400
You have a huge amount of data.

11:32.700 --> 11:33.320
A huge amount.

11:33.720 --> 11:35.940
But you forgot to write down the oven temperature.

11:36.800 --> 11:42.920
You compressed the event of baking, but you threw away the single most vital data point for the query,

11:43.240 --> 11:44.120
how do I bake this?

11:44.820 --> 11:47.760
Having lots of data doesn't save you if it's the wrong data.

11:47.940 --> 11:48.200
Right.

11:48.480 --> 11:51.600
And you can't just guess the oven temperature based on the weight of the flour.

11:51.600 --> 11:53.760
No, because the fiber is too wide.

11:54.060 --> 11:56.440
The flour weight doesn't narrow down the temperature possibilities.

11:57.260 --> 11:59.140
Finally, there is the third error.

12:00.420 --> 12:01.220
Approximation error.

12:01.820 --> 12:05.460
This is also an architectural failure, but it's a failure of precision.

12:05.940 --> 12:09.300
Meaning you have the right data, but your tools are blunt.

12:09.580 --> 12:09.900
Yes.

12:10.300 --> 12:15.760
Your query might be perfectly compressible, and your representation might have saved the exact right variables,

12:15.760 --> 12:21.200
but the query itself is too mathematically complex to be executed accurately by your system.

12:21.640 --> 12:23.680
So it's like having an oven that only has two settings.

12:24.500 --> 12:25.960
Incinerate and lukewarm.

12:26.540 --> 12:27.660
That's a great way to put it.

12:27.920 --> 12:32.980
You wrote down that you need exactly 375 degrees so you avoided the sufficiency error.

12:33.480 --> 12:35.540
And the universe allows bread to be baked.

12:36.120 --> 12:37.580
No compressibility error.

12:38.140 --> 12:42.880
But your instrument lacks the precision to actually execute the query.

12:43.360 --> 12:45.080
You suffer an approximation error.

12:45.080 --> 12:51.440
When you look at the fundamental error decomposition, the most profound takeaway is their absolute independence.

12:52.620 --> 12:53.840
Explain what you mean by that.

12:53.900 --> 12:59.360
Well, you can build an AI model with flawless precision, completely eliminating approximation error.

12:59.560 --> 13:04.740
You can perfectly design its architecture to avoid sufficiency error, saving all the right variables.

13:05.000 --> 13:05.180
Okay.

13:05.420 --> 13:12.040
But if the world it operates in is fundamentally unstable and chaotic, you will still fail due to compressibility error.

13:12.040 --> 13:17.080
You can engineer your way out of the last two, but you cannot engineer your way out of the first.

13:17.360 --> 13:19.920
Which means we are entirely at the mercy of the universe.

13:20.200 --> 13:20.680
Completely.

13:20.680 --> 13:27.800
If compressibility is this hard structural limit of reality, we really need to know what makes a history compressible in the first place.

13:28.220 --> 13:30.900
Why does the universe let us summarize some things but not others?

13:30.900 --> 13:34.560
And this brings us to the absolute core of Fliction's theory.

13:35.020 --> 13:35.500
Stability.

13:35.940 --> 13:36.460
Stability.

13:37.100 --> 13:38.340
This is the secret engine.

13:38.460 --> 13:41.220
It's the secret engine of reality that makes knowledge possible.

13:41.620 --> 13:49.760
The Stomerity Hypothesis states that for any system to be compressible, the influence of the past must decay with what the paper calls interaction order.

13:50.040 --> 13:50.700
Interaction order.

13:50.700 --> 13:53.260
I want to make sure we really nail this down because it's so critical.

13:53.260 --> 13:56.980
Think of interaction order as the complexity of the what-ifs.

13:57.740 --> 13:59.400
A first-order interaction is simple.

13:59.840 --> 14:02.400
Event A directly causes event B.

14:02.680 --> 14:04.700
Like me kicking a ball and the ball moving.

14:05.020 --> 14:05.500
Exactly.

14:06.000 --> 14:08.180
A high-order interaction is complex.

14:08.480 --> 14:15.760
How does the relationship between event A and event B change if event C happens, but only while event D is absent?

14:15.920 --> 14:16.460
Oh, wow.

14:16.760 --> 14:16.960
Okay.

14:17.020 --> 14:19.860
It's the deeply tangled web of historical context.

14:19.860 --> 14:27.380
Stability requires that as time moves forward, those hyper-complex, high-order historical interactions matter less and less to the present state.

14:27.720 --> 14:33.240
The universe literally has to let the tangled details fade away, leaving only the broad strokes.

14:33.400 --> 14:33.660
Yes.

14:34.060 --> 14:40.620
And Fliction maps out how this decay manifests across different domains, calling them the three faces of stability.

14:40.820 --> 14:41.460
Let's go through them.

14:41.660 --> 14:44.420
In human memory, stability shows up as forgetting.

14:44.420 --> 14:50.280
We lose sensitivity to distant, complex, experiential context.

14:51.240 --> 14:56.260
In semantic reasoning, how we build concepts in language stability manifests as abstraction.

14:56.500 --> 14:57.460
Abstraction, right.

14:57.920 --> 15:03.340
We absorb incredibly complex, lower-level exceptions into coarser, higher-level rules.

15:03.420 --> 15:11.460
Like how we learn the concept of a chair without needing to remember every specific arrangement of four legs in a seat we've ever seen in our entire lives.

15:11.580 --> 15:12.360
That makes total sense.

15:12.360 --> 15:16.220
And in physical systems, stability manifests as relaxation.

15:17.080 --> 15:24.360
A physical field loses sensitivity to the exact microscopic initial conditions that started it, settling into a broad macrostate.

15:25.020 --> 15:28.140
But the text outlines that this decay doesn't always happen the same way.

15:28.420 --> 15:32.460
There are actually three possible outcomes for how a universe handles its past.

15:32.580 --> 15:32.840
Right.

15:33.100 --> 15:34.120
Outcome A, B, and C.

15:34.300 --> 15:36.220
Let's start with outcome A, exponential stability.

15:36.320 --> 15:39.920
In outcome A, the influence of high-order history drops off a cliff.

15:40.220 --> 15:41.620
It decays exponentially.

15:41.620 --> 15:42.200
As decay.

15:42.360 --> 15:43.060
Very fast.

15:43.420 --> 15:45.780
This is the dream scenario for scientists.

15:46.220 --> 15:52.940
It means that to predict the future or reconstruct the near past, you don't need to know the entire history of the universe.

15:53.540 --> 15:57.020
You only need a very small, highly compressed representation.

15:57.020 --> 16:04.840
In a universe with exponential stability, science is easy, models are efficient, and counterfactuals are highly recoverable.

16:05.000 --> 16:05.440
Exactly.

16:05.860 --> 16:08.940
Then there is outcome B, polynomial stability.

16:09.140 --> 16:10.100
And how is that different?

16:10.100 --> 16:11.100
Here, the decay still happens.

16:11.100 --> 16:13.220
Here, the decay still happens, but it has a heavy tail.

16:13.440 --> 16:14.100
It's slow.

16:14.460 --> 16:17.160
History clings to the present for a much longer time.

16:17.240 --> 16:18.300
Doesn't drop off a cliff.

16:18.300 --> 16:20.380
No, it gradually slopes down.

16:20.380 --> 16:27.460
The high-order interactions do eventually fade, but you have to go much deeper into the historical record to get accurate answers.

16:27.460 --> 16:32.200
So, in this universe, compression is possible, but it's incredibly costly.

16:32.780 --> 16:34.320
You need massive amounts of data.

16:35.020 --> 16:37.160
Science is possible, but it's hard.

16:37.280 --> 16:37.780
Precisely.

16:38.160 --> 16:39.700
And then, outcome C.

16:39.840 --> 16:40.840
The nightmare scenario.

16:40.900 --> 16:41.860
The nightmare scenario.

16:42.120 --> 16:42.800
No stability.

16:42.980 --> 16:43.920
Meaning no decay at all.

16:43.920 --> 16:44.480
None.

16:44.960 --> 16:52.960
The microscopic, high-order historical interactions remain just as influential thousands of years later as they were the moment they happened.

16:53.620 --> 16:57.000
In outcome C, compression is structurally impossible.

16:57.640 --> 17:00.460
To know anything, you have to know absolutely everything.

17:00.680 --> 17:02.100
If I'm following the logic here.

17:02.200 --> 17:02.460
Yeah.

17:02.460 --> 17:12.940
If our universe was an outcome C universe, where things never naturally abstract or relax or fade, then explanation itself would be literally impossible.

17:12.940 --> 17:15.640
Consider what an explanation actually is.

17:15.900 --> 17:19.140
When you explain why a window broke, you say a baseball hit it.

17:19.220 --> 17:19.420
Right.

17:19.680 --> 17:21.340
That is a massive compression.

17:21.660 --> 17:25.700
